"A heap out of bounds read occurs in JSLexer::consumeBracedCodePoint due to continued use of the curCharPtr_ pointer after it is set to the end of the buffer. This situation arises when an invalid code point triggers a call to forceEOF in JSLexer::error, which sets curCharPtr_ to the buffer's end, but JSLexer::consumeBracedCodePoint does not properly handle this state and reads past the buffer boundary."

Let me re-examine the loop in `consumeBracedCodePoint`:

```cpp
for (; *curCharPtr_ != '}'; ++curCharPtr_) {
    int ch = *curCharPtr_;
    // ... 
    else {
      if (curCharPtr_ == bufferEnd_) {
        // ... handle end of buffer
        return llvm::None;
      }
      // Invalid character
      if (!failed && errorOnFail) {
        error(...);  // forceEOF sets curCharPtr_ = bufferEnd_
      }
      failed = true;
      continue;  // Skips ++curCharPtr_
    }
    // ...
}
```

After `forceEOF()` sets `curCharPtr_ = bufferEnd_`, the `continue` goes to the next iteration. `*curCharPtr_` is `\0` (at bufferEnd_), which is not `}`, so the loop body executes. `ch = *curCharPtr_` = 0, falls into else, checks `curCharPtr_ == bufferEnd_` which is true, and returns `llvm::None`. So this path seems safe.

But wait - what if `error()` is called but does NOT call `forceEOF()` (because error limit not reached)? Then `curCharPtr_` stays where it is, `continue` skips `++curCharPtr_`, and the same character is processed again. This would be an infinite loop.

Unless... the error limit IS reached. Let me check if there's a way to set the error limit low. Let me look at the fuzzer setup:

